High-Level Summary

You built an AI-assisted operational intelligence and analytics platform on top of distributed telemetry and reporting data stored in ClickHouse.

The goal was to help customer experience (CX), operations, and internal teams query enterprise engagement data in natural language instead of manually writing SQL queries or navigating dashboards.

What Problem Were You Solving?

Different enterprise customers generated huge amounts of operational and engagement telemetry:

Active users
Feature adoption
Accent translation usage
Synthesis counts
Workflow engagement
Customer-specific operational metrics
Jira issue correlations
Troubleshooting signals

The problem was:

CX/support teams were not technical
Writing SQL queries repeatedly was slow
Existing dashboards were rigid
Troubleshooting across distributed systems was difficult

So you built an AI/LLM-assisted analytics layer that converted human questions into structured analytical queries.

System Architecture (Simple Explanation)
1. Data Ingestion Layer

Telemetry and reporting data from multiple services flowed into pipelines.

Example events:

User login activity
Translation requests
Accent synthesis events
Feature usage
Customer workflow interactions
Service logs
Operational metrics

These events were aggregated and stored in ClickHouse because it is optimized for:

High-ingestion analytics workloads
Fast aggregations
Time-series querying
Large-scale reporting queries
2. ClickHouse as the Analytics Backend

You designed reporting tables that stored:

Customer-level metrics
Group-level engagement
Feature adoption statistics
Active user counts
Time-based analytics
Operational workflow metrics

Example schema idea:

customer_id
group_id
feature_name
active_users
translation_requests
synthesis_count
timestamp

Since ClickHouse is columnar, aggregation queries like:

COUNT(DISTINCT users)
SUM(synthesis_count)
GROUP BY customer/group

were extremely fast even on large datasets.

What You Mean by “Dictionary”

What you’re calling a “dictionary” is likely one of these:

Metadata mapping layer
Query abstraction layer
Intent-to-query mapping
Semantic dictionary
Dynamic query templates

Basically:

You mapped business terminology used by CX teams into actual database fields and query logic.

Example:

CX Question	Internal Mapping
“How many active users in Group A?”	COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)
“How many used accent translation?”	SUM(accent_translation_events)
“Most engaged customer workflows?”	Aggregation query over workflow events

So instead of exposing raw schema details, you created a semantic layer.

That’s actually a strong architecture concept.

Where LLM/LangChain Came In

You likely used LangChain or similar orchestration to:

Parse natural language questions
Identify customer/group/feature
Map intent to analytical query
Generate safe SQL/query templates
Execute against ClickHouse
Return summarized insights

Example flow:

User asks:

“How many active users in the Accent Translation group last week?”

LLM extracts:

{
  "metric": "active_users",
  "feature": "accent_translation",
  "time_range": "last_week"
}

Then backend generates optimized ClickHouse query.

